Fed by the Santa Ana River, Lytle Creek and Mill Creek, the basin provides drinking water for the cities of San Bernardino and Riverside and surrounding communities.
Water percolates underground and is stored in a basin with a capacity of 5 million acre feet. One acre foot provides enough water for two families for a year.
In 2005, winter storms deluged the basin to the point where water had to be pumped out. Officials worried the water-laden soil could lead to liquefaction, or a sudden weakening of the earth��s holding power during an earthquake that could cause buildings to collapse.
